    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "title",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "# Workshop results: from feature breadth to scenario fit"
      },
      {
        "id": "technical_summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "combined_analysis",
        "body": "## The short version\n\n- **Task 2 produced a broad, experience-led feature space.** Media/play, navigation/mobility, and ambient/interface concepts each account for 7 of 40 cards. Media/play is the only domain present in all four groups.\n- **Task 3 sharply separated safety from leisure.** All four safety concepts were assigned to high-demand driving, while all five media/play concepts were assigned to low-demand driving.\n- **Six concepts were explicitly relevant to both scenarios.** These centered cards cover vehicle feedback, destination context, decision support, calm presentation, ambient visuals, and connected information.\n- **Task 3 was not sorting alone.** Twenty-five of 37 normalized concepts directly trace to T2; the remaining twelve were introduced, merged, or refined during scenario work."
      },
      {
        "id": "t2_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "t2_evidence",
        "body": "## T2 explored experience as much as utility\n\nThe largest domains were **media/play, navigation/mobility, and ambient/interface mode**, each with seven cards. Cross-group presence adds an important second view: media/play appeared in all four groups, while navigation, ambient/interface, communication, and vehicle state each appeared in three. Safety generated only three cards across two groups, so card volume alone would understate its later importance."
      },
      {
        "id": "t2_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "t2_domains_chart"
      },
      {
        "id": "t2_table_block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "t2_summary_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "t3_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "t3_evidence",
        "body": "## T3 turned breadth into a workload-sensitive information hierarchy\n\nThe scenario canvas produced the clearest pattern in the analysis. **Safety was high-only and media/play was low-only.** Communication was mostly low-demand, with four of five concepts placed there. Navigation remained flexible, with four high-demand concepts, one low-demand concept, and one concept spanning both. Connected assistance also crossed all three scenario states, suggesting that appropriateness depends on the assistant function rather than the technology label alone."
      },
      {
        "id": "t3_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "t3_scenario_chart"
      },
      {
        "id": "t3_table_block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "t3_matrix_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "group_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "t3_evidence",
        "body": "## The groups followed different scenario strategies\n\nG1 used high, low, and both states. G2 and G3 made a binary high/low split. G4 took a different route: it consolidated fifteen T2 cards into three broad bundles and placed every bundle on the central axis, indicating relevance in both scenarios. This variation is analytically meaningful and should not be flattened into one workshop-wide ranking."
      },
      {
        "id": "group_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "group_scenario_chart"
      },
      {
        "id": "priority_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "t3_evidence",
        "body": "## Closest-to-driver placements reinforce the scenario split\n\nAt rank 1 in high-demand driving, G1 and G3 prioritized collision or unsafe-action alerts; G3 also placed car status there, while G2 prioritized parking and mood background. In low-demand driving, the closest tier is dominated by music, personal media, messages, assistants, smart-home information, and topography. Ranks are ordinal within each group and scenario, so they indicate local priority rather than a shared distance scale."
      },
      {
        "id": "priority_table_block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "priority_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "lineage_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "t3_evidence",
        "body": "## T3 continued ideation rather than merely sorting T2\n\nTwenty-five normalized T3 concepts directly match a T2 card. Eight concepts are new, three consolidate multiple T2 cards, and one refines an earlier idea. This means the tasks form a design progression: T2 establishes a candidate space, while T3 both evaluates scenario fit and further develops that space."
      },
      {
        "id": "lineage_chart_block",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "lineage_chart"
      },
      {
        "id": "scope_methods",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "combined_analysis",
        "body": "## How the analysis was built\n\nThe evidence was transcribed card by card and coded with a nine-domain codebook. One primary domain supplies mutually exclusive headline counts; secondary domains preserve cross-domain meaning. Centered T3 cards are coded as **both**, following the confirmed workshop interpretation. Vertical rows are treated as tied ordinal proximity tiers. One exact duplicate ETA and destination-weather card is retained for audit but excluded from normalized counts."
      },
      {
        "id": "codebook_table_block",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "codebook_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "limitations",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "combined_analysis",
        "body": "## What these findings do and do not establish\n\n- Counts describe **group-produced artefacts**, not the prevalence of preferences among 13 independent participants.\n- Coding is an analyst-generated first pass. Ambiguous and bundled cards carry lower confidence in the evidence ledgers.\n- The T3 boards support scenario state and ordinal proximity, not precise spatial distance.\n- Counting the retained duplicate changes low-demand from 15 to 16 raw cards, but does not alter the safety, media, communication, or both-scenario conclusions.\n- No claim in this report uses the earlier spreadsheet clustering or analysis screenshots."
      },
      {
        "id": "next_steps",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Recommended next step\n\nUse this as the auditable first coding pass. Before thesis publication, review the low-confidence cards and the T2→T3 lineage judgments with a second reader, then freeze the codebook and regenerate the report. For Chapter 4, lead with the safety-versus-leisure scenario split, preserve the six both-scenario concepts, and describe T3 as continued concept development rather than a mechanical prioritization exercise."
      }
    ]
  },
